# Line endings
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# This repository is developed on Windows (core.autocrlf=true) and DEPLOYED on
# Linux. Without this file, every text file is committed with CRLF and a shell
# script checked out on the VPS fails at the shebang:
#
#     ./deploy.sh: /usr/bin/env: bad interpreter: No such file or directory
#
# — which is a confusing way to say "there is a \r on the end of the line".
# The same applies to anything the Linux containers execute or parse.

# Default: let git normalise text in the repository, check out natively.
* text=auto

# Anything Linux executes or parses MUST be LF, on every platform.
*.sh            text eol=lf
Dockerfile      text eol=lf
*.conf          text eol=lf
*.yml           text eol=lf
*.yaml          text eol=lf
.env.template   text eol=lf
.gitkeep        text eol=lf

# Executable bit for the deploy entrypoint, so a fresh clone can run it without
# a chmod first. (Git tracks only this one permission bit.)
deploy.sh       text eol=lf

# Binary — never touch.
*.png           binary
*.jpg           binary
# Fonts are embedded into rendered certificates by backend/services/
# certificateRenderer.js. Left to the text=auto heuristic a single mangled byte
# turns into a font resvg silently refuses to load, and certificates render in a
# fallback face on the server that produced them — declare them binary outright.
*.ttf           binary
*.otf           binary
*.woff          binary
*.woff2         binary
*.jpeg          binary
*.gif           binary
*.ico           binary
*.pdf           binary
*.woff          binary
*.woff2         binary
